PHASE 0 — MINDSET (before reading a single line of code):
  - Assume there IS a bug. Your job is to find it, not prove the code is safe.
  - Never report positive findings ("this looks safe"). Report ONLY findings.
  - Read every file MORE THAN ONCE. First pass = understand. Second pass = attack.
  - Connect findings: a LOW severity finding + another LOW = possible CRITICAL
  - Follow data flows: LLM output → tool call → real action. Assume the LLM
    output itself can be attacker-controlled (via prompt injection).
  - Ask "what happens if the model hallucinates or is manipulated into
    calling this with malicious arguments?" for every tool/function.
  - Ask "what happens if two agents/threads act on shared state at once?"
  - Ask "can untrusted content (web page, doc, tool result, MCP server
    response) end up executed as an instruction?"

PHASE 1 — ARCHITECTURE MAP (you are here now)
  - Build a complete picture of what exists before you dig into any one thing
  - Map every package/module, every public interface
  - Find where trust boundaries exist (user input, LLM output, tool output,
    MCP server responses, retrieved documents)
  - Find where external data enters the system
  - Find where sensitive operations happen: code execution, shell/process
    spawning, file read/write, HTTP requests, credential use, agent handoff

PHASE 2 — ATTACK SURFACE PRIORITIZATION
  - Rank areas by: (a) impact if broken + (b) complexity = most likely bugs
  - Focus on: tool/function-calling dispatch > code execution tools >
    HTTP/network tools > file tools > MCP integration > memory/state >
    multi-agent orchestration > utilities

PHASE 3 — DEEP DIVE (per targeted area)
  - Read the full function, trace all callers, trace all callees
  - Build a call graph mentally: who calls this? what does this call?
  - Build a data flow: where does this input come from (is it LLM-
    controlled)? where does it go (does it reach a dangerous sink)?
  - Find the ROOT CAUSE, not just the symptom

PHASE 4 — ROOT CAUSE CHAIN BUILDING
  - Connect low/info findings into chains: F1 → F7 → F12 → CRITICAL
  - Example chain: tool output not sanitized [INFO] + tool output re-fed as
    instruction [LOW] + no allowlist on shell tool [MED] = remote code
    execution via indirect prompt injection [CRITICAL]
  - Document every link in the chain with file:line references

PHASE 5 — CONFIRMATION & POC
  - Confirm the bug is real in the actual code (double check, not assumption)
  - Build a PoC: exact steps, inputs (including a sample malicious prompt or
    tool payload if relevant), and expected vs actual behavior

